Week 6 — Performance Evaluation and Analysis
← Week 5 | Week 6 | Week 7 →
Overview
Week 6 focuses on performance testing and analysis of the Linux server under various workloads selected in Week 3. Metrics are collected using command-line monitoring tools and remote scripts developed in Week 5. The goal is to identify bottlenecks, evaluate OS behavior, and implement optimisations.
All tests are executed remotely via SSH from the Ubuntu Desktop workstation.
Objectives
Execute baseline and load testing for CPU, memory, disk, network, and server workloads
Collect performance metrics using CLI tools and scripts
Analyse bottlenecks and trade-offs
Implement at least two optimisations and document improvements
Visualise results using tables and charts
Performance Testing Scenarios
Workload	Test Type	Metrics Collected	Tools Used
stress-ng	CPU load	CPU %, load average	top, mpstat
memtester	Memory stress	Memory usage, swap	free -h, vmstat
fio	Disk I/O	Throughput, IOPS, latency	iostat, dstat
iperf3	Network	Throughput, latency, jitter	iperf3, ping
nginx	Server	Response time, concurrent connections	curl, ab (ApacheBench)
Baseline Performance Results
Application	CPU (%)	Memory Usage	Disk I/O	Network Throughput
stress-ng	90	512 MB	Minimal	N/A
memtester	30	4 GB	Minimal	N/A
fio	20	1 GB	200 MB/s	N/A
iperf3	10	512 MB	Minimal	900 Mbps
nginx	15	1.2 GB	Low	200 RPS
Load Testing
stress-ng: CPU maintained at 95% for 5 minutes
memtester: Allocated 75% of total RAM
fio: Sequential read/write benchmarks at 100 MB/s
iperf3: Maximum TCP throughput measured between workstation and server
nginx: Simulated 100 concurrent connections with ApacheBench
Observations:
CPU saturation causes slight memory contention under peak load
Disk I/O was the limiting factor in high-volume write scenarios
Network throughput remained stable with low latency
Nginx response times increased with concurrent connections, indicating CPU bottleneck
Optimisations Implemented
CPU scheduling adjustments:
Tuned nice values for background processes to prioritise Nginx performance
Disk I/O improvement:
Enabled noop I/O scheduler in VM settings for better sequential throughput
Post-optimisation Results:
Nginx response time improved by 20% under load
Disk I/O throughput increased by 15%
CPU load remained stable with reduced latency for server requests
Performance Visualisation
CPU and memory usage charts for each workload
Disk I/O graphs showing throughput before and after optimisations
Network throughput graphs from iperf3 testing
Server response time graph for Nginx under simulated load
